<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rogun Dam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rogun Dam is a hydroelectric dam on the Vakhsh river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, designed to be among the tallest in the world at about 335 metres. Begun in the Soviet period in 1976, it was halted after independence; work resumed in 2016, and its first turbines began generating power in 2018. Downstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> long opposed it over fears for its water supply.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rogun-dam.jpg" alt="Rogun Dam" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sosh19632 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rogun_Dam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rogun Dam was first proposed in 1959 and a technical scheme was developed by 1965. It stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhsh-river">Vakhsh River</a> about 110 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> as one of the planned plants of the Vakhsh Cascade. Construction began in 1976 but stalled after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and was abandoned in 1993, when a flood destroyed the early works, and only preliminary construction had been done over three decades.</p><p>An agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1994 was never implemented and was denounced by the Tajik parliament. In 2004 an agreement was signed with RUSAL to finish the dam, and in 2007 a partnership with Russia was announced, but Russia later refused it over a dispute about the controlling stake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The dam was long listed as the highest in the world at 335 metres, but this was only ever the projected height; the structure stood only about 200 feet high until 1993. In 2014 three designs were under consideration: the original 335 metres and alternatives of 300 and 265 metres. The power plant is planned to have six turbines with a combined capacity of 3600 MW and to produce about 17 terawatt-hours a year.</p><p>In 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> launched a public share offering to raise US$1.4 billion and collected US$184 million. Work was suspended in August 2012 pending World Bank studies, whose environmental assessment of 2014 stated that most impacts were small and easily mitigated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 1 July 2016 the state commission chose the Italian company Salini Impregilo for a contract of US$3.9 billion. On 29 October 2016 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emomali-rahmon">Emomali Rahmon</a> launched construction and the river was diverted through reconstructed tunnels. The first unit was commissioned in November 2018 and the second in September 2019, both at a lower head, and concrete pouring on the main dam core began in July 2022.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, which feared for its irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, opposed the project from Soviet times. In 2010 its president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a>, called it a stupid project, but in July 2018 its foreign minister said that Uzbekistan would not oppose it, provided that guarantees under signed conventions were kept.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rogun Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy depends on aluminium, cotton, hydropower such as the <b>Rogun dam</b>, and remittances from migrants in Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></li></ul></section>
